Ansible Roles

Today I learned about Ansible Roles, which help organize Playbooks into reusable and modular components 📦
Instead of writing one large Playbook, Roles allow us to structure automation in a clean and scalable way.
 Think of Roles as reusable building blocks for automation 🧱

🔹 📁 Role Structure
An Ansible Role follows a predefined directory structure:
• tasks/
 • handlers/
 • vars/
 • defaults/
 • files/
 • templates/
This structure makes automation organized, readable, and production-ready 🚀

🔹 ⚙️ tasks, handlers & vars
📌 tasks/ → Contains the main list of tasks to execute
 📌 handlers/ → Contains handlers triggered by notify
 📌 vars/ → Stores role-specific variables
These components work together to automate configurations in a structured way.

🔹 🧩 defaults
The defaults/ directory stores default variable values for the role.
These variables have the lowest precedence and can be easily overridden.
 It helps in making roles flexible and customizable 🔄

🔹 📂 files & templates
📌 files/ → Used to store static files (configs, scripts, etc.)
 📌 templates/ → Used for dynamic files with variables (Jinja2 templates)
Templates allow dynamic configuration based on environment and variables 🌍
This makes Roles highly reusable across different servers and environments.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Roles make Playbooks modular and reusable.
 Structured folders improve automation management.
 Reusable roles = scalable DevOps automation.
